  "final_report": "[stop_reason: hop_cap_reached | hops used: 4/4]\n\nAnalyst summary:\n**Investigation\u202fsummary \u2013 Disruption at Xiamen Tungsten (first reported\u202f\u2248\u202f22\u202fSept\u202f2010)**  \n\n---\n\n### 1. Companies that are structurally \u201cat\u2011risk\u201d\n\nThe **down\u2011stream supply\u2011graph traversal** (max\u202ftier\u202f=\u202f2) identified every publicly\u2011known downstream customer or alternative supplier that is linked to Xiamen Tungsten.  \nThese firms are the ones that could feel a material impact if Xiamen Tungsten\u2019s tungsten output were curtailed.\n\n#### Tier\u202f1 (direct downstream links \u2013 highest confidence)\n\n| Company | Primary business (relevant to tungsten) |\n|---------|-------------------------------------------|\n| **Gotion High\u2011Tech** (China) \u2013 lithium\u2011iron\u2011phosphate battery cells |\n| **Murata Manufacturing** (Japan) \u2013 multilayer ceramic capacitors (MLCCs) |\n| **CALB** (China) \u2013 EV lithium\u2011ion battery cells |\n| **Contemporary Amperex Technology** (CATL, China) \u2013 EV lithium\u2011ion battery cells |\n| **Sunwoda Electronic** (China) \u2013 lithium\u2011ion cells & packs |\n| **Sumitomo Electric Industries** (Japan) \u2013 automotive wiring harnesses |\n| **TDK** (Japan) \u2013 passive components (capacitors/inductors) |\n| **Hunan Corun New Energy** (China) \u2013 Ni\u2011MH battery cells |\n| **Samsung\u202fSDI** (South Korea) \u2013 lithium\u2011ion cells |\n| **Zhejiang Founder Motor** (China) \u2013 EV drive/micro\u2011motors |\n| **Panasonic** (Japan) \u2013 automotive lithium\u2011ion cells |\n| **BYD** (China) \u2013 battery\u2011electric vehicles |\n\n*(The full Tier\u202f1 list contains 12 companies; the table shows the most material ones.)*\n\n#### Tier\u202f2 (customers of Tier\u202f1 firms or alternative suppliers \u2013 still high confidence)\n\nA very large set of downstream users was returned (over 150 entities). The most notable groups include:\n\n* **Automotive OEMs** \u2013 Toyota, Volkswagen, General Motors, Ford, Hyundai Motor, Nissan, Honda, Kia, Mazda, Subaru, Renault, Stellantis, SAIC Motor, Geely, BYD\u202fAuto, NIO, Li Auto, XPeng, Lucid, Rivian, Lordstown Motors, Polestar, Volvo Cars, Mercedes\u2011Benz, Daimler Truck, etc.  \n* **Battery & EV\u2011component makers** \u2013 Samsung Electronics (auto semiconductors), Eve Energy, Romeo Power, Magna International, Aisin, BorgWarner, Denso, ZF Friedrichshafen, Valeo\u2011type suppliers, etc.  \n* **Materials & chemicals providers** \u2013 CNGR Advanced Material, Umicore, Ningbo Shanshan (graphite anodes), Xinxiang Tianli Energy (cathode materials), Gem (battery recycling), etc.  \n* **Industrial & construction firms** \u2013 China Petroleum & Chemical, Taiwan Cement, State Grid Corp of China, Obayashi, etc.  \n\nAll of these companies appear in the traversal output with a confidence rating of *high* or *medium*, indicating that they are linked to Xiamen Tungsten through at least one supply\u2011chain hop (directly for Tier\u202f1, indirectly for Tier\u202f2).\n\n> **Bottom line:**\u202fIf Xiamen Tungsten\u2019s production were disrupted, the above\u2011listed firms \u2013 especially those in Tier\u202f1 and the most prominent Tier\u202f2 OEMs and battery makers \u2013 are the ones that could experience material supply constraints or need to source alternative tungsten.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Evidence gathered for specific companies\n\n| Company | Tool(s) consulted | Findings / evidence |\n|---------|-------------------|----------------------|\n| **Tesla** | `get_supplier_info` (filings searched up to\u202f22\u202fSept\u202f2010) | No filings on record dated on or before the event date \u2013 therefore no direct SEC\u2011filing evidence of a tungsten exposure. |\n| **Murata Manufacturing** | `get_supplier_info` | No filings on record for Murata \u2013 cannot locate any self\u2011disclosed information about a tungsten supply issue. |\n| **Gotion High\u2011Tech** | `get_supplier_info` | No filings on record for Gotion \u2013 no direct filing evidence available. |\n\n*No other companies were checked with `get_supplier_info` or `search_corpus` because the tool\u2011call budget has been exhausted.*\n\n---\n\n### 3. What could not be confirmed\n\n| Company / group | Reason for lack of confirmation |\n|-----------------|----------------------------------|\n| **All Tier\u202f1 and Tier\u202f2 firms** (except the three listed above) \u2013 e.g., Toyota, Volkswagen, Samsung\u202fSDI, BYD, etc. | No SEC\u2011filing excerpts or corpus mentions were retrieved; we have only the structural link from the supply\u2011graph traversal. |\n| **Murata Manufacturing & Gotion High\u2011Tech** | No public filings exist in the database for these entities (or none covering the 2010\u201109\u201122 window). |\n| **Tesla** | Filings exist but none dated on or before the disruption date, so no contemporaneous disclosure can be cited. |\n| **Any downstream impact beyond Tier\u202f2** | The investigation was limited to two tiers; deeper links were not explored and therefore remain unverified. |\n\n---\n\n### 4. Overall assessment (based solely on available evidence)\n\n*The supply\u2011graph traversal provides a comprehensive, data\u2011driven map of which companies are *structurally dependent* on Xiamen Tungsten.*  \nBecause no contemporaneous SEC filings or corpus mentions were obtainable for the three sample companies we examined, **the only concrete proof of exposure is the graph\u2011derived relationship itself**.  \n\nConsequently:\n\n* **High\u2011confidence at\u2011risk firms** \u2013 all Tier\u202f1 entities (e.g., Murata, Gotion, CALB, CATL, Sunwoda, Sumitomo Electric, TDK, Samsung\u202fSDI, BYD) are directly downstream and would need to replace tungsten inputs or face production delays.  \n* **Major automotive OEMs and battery manufacturers** \u2013 appear in Tier\u202f2; they could feel secondary effects (e.g., higher component costs, redesign of parts that use tungsten\u2011based alloys or ceramics).  \n* **No direct public disclosures** were found for any of the sampled companies around the 2010\u201109\u201122 event, meaning that if a disruption did occur, it was either not material enough to be disclosed in filings at that time, or the relevant disclosures are simply absent from the searchable database.\n\n---\n\n### 5. Recommendations for further verification (outside the current tool budget)\n\n1. **Targeted document search** \u2013 run `search_corpus` with each high\u2011impact company name (e.g., Toyota, Samsung\u202fSDI, BYD) to locate any mention of \u201ctungsten\u201d, \u201cXiamen Tungsten\u201d, or related supply\u2011chain issues in 2010\u20112011 filings.  \n2. **Industry news & press releases** \u2013 consult external news archives for the September\u2011October\u202f2010 period for reports on Xiamen Tungsten\u2019s outage and any statements from downstream customers.  \n3. **Direct supplier questionnaires** \u2013 reach out to the Tier\u202f1 firms (Murata, Gotion, CALB, etc.) for confirmation of tungsten sourcing arrangements at that time.\n\n---\n\n**Conclusion:**  \nBased on the available graph data, a large number of battery\u2011cell producers, electronic component makers, and automotive OEMs are structurally linked to Xiamen Tungsten and therefore potentially exposed to its 2010 disruption. Direct documentary evidence (SEC filings) for these exposures could not be retrieved within the current tool limits; only the structural connections can be confirmed at this stage. Further targeted searches would be required to substantiate actual operational impacts.\n\nCOVERAGE GAPS (105) -- reported explicitly, not omitted:\n  - gotion high-tech: no evidence gathered\n  - murata manufacturing: no evidence gathered\n  - calb: no evidence gathered\n  - contemporary amperex technology: no evidence gathered\n  - sunwoda electronic: no evidence gathered\n  - sumitomo electric ind